  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\u223cN(\\mu ,{6}^{2})$ with $P(X<5)=0.7734,$ find $\\mu .$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ | $\\,0.8051\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu =$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5)=0.7734\\,\u21d2\\,P(X\\le 5)=0.7734\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =0.7734 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{5-\\mu }{6}) & =0.7734 \\\\ \u03a6(\\frac{5-\\mu }{6}) & =0.7734 \\\\ \\frac{5-\\mu }{6} & ={\u03a6}^{-1}(0.7734)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\u03a6(0.75)=0.7734\\,\u21d2\\,{\u03a6}^{-1}(0.7734)=0.75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-\\mu }{6} & =0.75 \\\\ 5-\\mu  & =4.5 \\\\ \\mu  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
